-- title:
--   The directed graph of a configuration on an open set, and well-connectedness
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space carrying a configuration $\Gamma$, and let $U \subseteq E$. The graph $G[U]$ has all of $E$ as vertex set; there is a **directed edge** from $x$ to $y$ when
--
--   $$x \in U \quad\text{and}\quad y \in V^\Gamma[x] = x + \Gamma(x),$$
--
--   so only edges issuing from vertices of $U$ are kept, while vertices outside $U$ may still be used as intermediate points. **Connectivity in $G[U]$** is understood in the underlying undirected graph: it is the reflexive–transitive closure of the relation "there is an edge from $a$ to $b$ or from $b$ to $a$".
--
--   A point $x$ is **well-connected in $U$** when some open neighbourhood of it is joined to it:
--
--   $$\exists\, W \text{ open},\quad x \in W \quad\text{and}\quad \forall p \in W,\ p \text{ is connected to } x \text{ in } G[U].$$
--
--   These three notions are the continuous prelude to the discrete chaining: the eventual connectivity theorem says that an open connected $U$ is a single connectivity class of $G[U]$, and the route to it is to show that every point of $U$ is well-connected, which makes the connectivity classes open, so that preconnectedness of $U$ finishes the argument.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Connectivity is `Relation.ReflTransGen`, which records that a path exists but not which one — sufficient here, but the reason later sections switch to `SimpleGraph.Walk`, whose length and edge list can be constrained. The file comment records two deviations: cone *types* are compared as sets rather than as axis–angle pairs, since the bundled double cone carries a unit axis rather than a point of projective space; and the induction closing the connectivity theorem is run on $B_r(x)\cap \tilde V[x]$, with the convex — hence connected — *half*-cone, because the source applies its inductive hypothesis to a set that is in general not connected, a double cone being a disjoint union of two half-cones.
